1 OCCU Brief Life History Biografía   Arias de Saavedra, Fernando. Señor de El Viso (II) y Castellar (II). Sevilla, c. 1450 – 1496. Alfaqueque mayor de Castilla.  Hijo de Juan Arias de Saavedra y de Juana de Avellaneda, en 1458, siendo m
2 CONC enor de edad, heredó
1 TITL Count
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Arias de Saavedra, Fernando. Lord of El Viso (II) and Castellar (II). Seville, c. 1450–1496. Major Alfaqueque of Castile. Son of Juan Arias de Saavedra and Juana de Avellaneda, in 1458, as a minor, he inherited the
2 CONC  estate established by his father two years earlier with the lordship of El Viso. In 1465, following the death of his brother Juan a few years earlier and the agreement to divide his father's property, he added the jurisdiction of
2 CONC  Castellar with certain responsibilities for his five sisters. In those early years and until he came of age, his house was effectively ruled by his uncle Gonzalo de Saavedra, the senior relative of the entire lineage in Seville.
2 CONT
2 CONT The town of Castellar, on the Granada border, and the main alfalfaquería of Castile constituted the part of his heritage that linked him to the border tradition of his lineage. Furthermore, the position of mayor of the strong cast
2 CONC le of Alcalá de Guadaira and the possession of one of the mayoralties of the land of Seville, with a voice and vote in its town council, assured him a prominent role in the turbulent Sevillian politics of the reign of Henry IV. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His marriage to Constanza Ponce de León, daughter of the Count of Arcos, brought him deeply involved in factional conflicts, always following the side of his brother-in-law Rodrigo Ponce de León. Like him, he supported the prince-
2 CONC king Alfonso between 1465 and 1468 and, later, stood out in the noble war between Ponces and Guzmáns from 1471 to 1474, contributing from Alcalá to the blockade imposed on Seville. On March 6, 1473, he achieved a resounding victor
2 CONC y in a cavalry engagement in which two brothers of the Duke of Medina Sidonia lost their lives. However, he lost Castellar in an unknown manner and time, as the peace treaty of 1474 stipulated its return.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like all his lineage, he initially supported Princess Juana against Isabella, to whom he was slow to obey. This led to the need for a royal pardon in 1476, followed in 1477 by the transfer of the mayorship of Alcalá de Guadaira. H
2 CONC owever, he retained and saw the remaining estate confirmed, even being able to expand the estate of his house with Castellar by power of March 7, 1492. This favor must have been related to his participation in various campaigns of
2 CONC  the Granada War, leading a distinguished troop of cavalry at his own expense.
2 CONT
2 CONT  In 1486, he was confirmed as the major alfalfaquería of Castile, although the monarchs did not make much use of his services and preferred to entrust the exchanges and ransoms of the numerous war captives to others.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hernán Arias de Saavedra married twice. The first time was to Constanza Ponce de León, who bore him three children; The second was with Catalina Ortiz de Guzmán, daughter of Juan Ortiz de Guzmán and Leonor de Esquivel, with whom h
2 CONC e had two more children. He died in 1496.
