1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Diogo Soares de Albergaria, also known as Diego Soares de Melo and the "Gallego" was a 16th-century Portuguese navigator and explorer.
2 CONT
2 CONT India: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Soares arrived to India circa 1538,[1] there he was a known murderer and pirate. Estêvão da Gama, by then Governor of Goa, issued a warrant for his arrest, but he was later granted a pardon by da Gama's successor, Martim Afonso de
2 CONC  Sousa,[2] who was Soares friend.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Under his command Soares commanded expeditions and at the same time that he continued his pirate activities near Portuguese Mozambique.
2 CONT
2 CONT Madagascar:
2 CONT
2 CONT Soares visited Madagascar in 1543 under the orders of Martim Afonso de Sousa, to investigate the whereabouts of de Sousa's brother who was reportedly wrecked there, the same year he returned to Kochi (the Cochin) without the infor
2 CONC mation but with loads of silver and slaves looted from the Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT The northern coastal town of Antsiranana was probably named after Soares, it contained his name until 1975, sometimes in a Spanish form as Diego Suárez also the large natural bay along the northeast coast of Madagascar is named Di
2 CONC ego Suarez Bay. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Burma:
2 CONT
2 CONT Soares landed in Portuguese Malacca in 1547,[1] driven by the weather.[2] There he stayed under the orders of Tabinshwehti, King of the Burmese of the Toungoo dynasty, where he became a wealthy man, worth four millions in jewels a
2 CONC nd other valuables, had a pension of 200,000 ducats yearly, was called the king's brother, and was supreme governor of the kingdom and general in chief of the army. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Burmese–Siamese War (1548) 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Burmese–Siamese War (1547–1549), Soares commanded a force of five captains and 180 professional mercenary,[5] he also led the failed invasion and siege of Tapuram, which ended after five months after 120,000 Peguans des
2 CONC erted when their leader Xemindoo (Smim Htaw) rebelled in their native Pegu, but also as a revenge of the mistreatments of Soares who was his general in chief.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death 
2 CONT
2 CONT While still a King Tabinshwehti's general, Soares tried to take off by force the daughter of a rich merchant, in the process he killed the groom and others who came to her rescue, and the bride committed suicide to avoid the disho
2 CONC nour. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Short after that, the King was killed and replaced by one of his generals called Zemin (Smim Sawhtut), who eventually handed Soares to the city of the disgraced bride, there the people stoned Soares to death, plundered his house, 
2 CONC and as much less treasure was found, he was believed to have buried the rest. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The episode of his death is described in the book Peregrinação by Fernão Mendes Pinto.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also[edit] Burmese–Siamese War (1547–49) Notes[edit] ^ Jump up to: a b c d e f Kerr, Volume 6, Chapter 4, Section 15 ^ Jump up to: a b Mendes Pinto, Pp. 620 ^ Barros and Couto, Pp. 180 ^ Jump up to: a b Van Den Boogaerd, Pp. 4
2 CONC 0-41 ^ Sodacan, Burmese–Siamese War Invasion References[edit] Mendes Pinto, Fernão (1989). Catz, Rebecca D (ed.). The Travels of Mendes Pinto. Chicago: The University of Chicago Press. ISBN 0-226-66951-3. Kerr, Robert (2004) [1st.
2 CONC  pub. Wm. Blackwood, Edinburgh & T. Cadell, London:1824]. A general history and collection of voyages and travels, arranged in systematic order: forming a complete history of the origin and progress of navigation, discovery and co
2 CONC mmerce, by sea and land, from the earliest ages to the present time. Chapel Hill, NC: Project Gutenberg. Retrieved 30 December 2012. do Couto, Diogo; de Barros, João & Severim de Faria, Manoel (1777–80). Da Asia de João de Barros,
2 CONC  dos feitos que os Portuguezes fizeram no descubrimento e conquista dos mares e terras do Oriente. Lisbon: Na Regia Officina Typografica Lisboa. Retrieved 30 December 2012. Van Den Boogaerd, Pierre (2009). Shipwrecks of Madagascar
2 CONC . New York: AEG Publishing Group. ISBN 978-1-60693-494-4.
2 CONT
