1 OCCU Biografía Don Sebastián Arias de Saavedra y Ortiz de Guzmán (ya mencionado como hijo del III Señor de Castellar y del Viso don Hernán Arias de Saavedra y Avellaneda en su segundo enlace con doña Leonor Ortiz de Guzmán), fué conoci
2 CONC do también por "
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Don Sebastian Arias de Saavedra y Ortiz de Guzman (already mentioned as son of the III Lord of Castellar and Viso, Don Hernan Arias de Saavedra y Avellaneda, in his second marriage with Doña Leonor Ortiz de Guzman)
2 CONC , was also known as "Sebastian Arias de Saavedra Guzman" and "Sebastian de Guzman Saavedra." He settled in Carmona, marrying Doña Isabel de Saravia or Sanabria, of the noble lineage of Sanabria and Sotomayor, as Dorantes de Carran
2 CONC za tells us in his "Brief account of the things of New Spain." They had as a son: Don Sebastian Arias de Saavedra-Guzman y Sanabria, a native of Seville and mentioned by Don Baltasar Dorantes de Carranza at number 69, page 311 of 
2 CONC his already cited work. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 11, 1512, and under the name "Sebastián de Sayavedra" (sic), he obtained authorization, along with other people, to go to America (entry 808, page 59, volume I of the "Catalogue of passengers to the Indies").
2 CONT
2 CONT "House of Sabastian de Saavedra. He came to this New Spain in the year 50, having come before from the kingdoms of Castile in the year 12 to the Island of Santo Domingo; and having gone with Captain Joan Ponce de Leon to the Islan
2 CONC d of San Juan de Puerto Rico, to pacify it, he spent the year 13 with Pedrarias Dávila, who came as Governor and Captain General to Darien and Nicaragua Mainland, going as Captain and lieutenant of the said Pedrarias; and having h
2 CONC elped to conquer all those provinces, populated the city of Panama and the town and port of Nombre de Dios, and came conquering and populating as far as Gracias a Dios, which is next to the provinces of Guatemala, passing those of
2 CONC  Honduras, and from there he went with Francisco Picarro, who later became Marquis, to the discovery of Peru, leaving a very good distribution in the province of Chuluteca, and was one of 13 companions who remained with Piçarro to
2 CONC  the discovery, and after discovery he went on to the conquest, and being captain in the pacification of those kingdoms, he made great raids at their expense and service."
2 CONT
2 CONT "This Sabastian de Saavedra was a native of Seville, son of Sabastian de Saavedra and Doña Isabel de Sanabria, a native of Carmona, of the Sanabrias and Sotomayores families of that town, noble people. Saavedra was one of those of
2 CONC  this surname from Seville. He married Doña Cathalina de Ocampo, legitimate daughter of Gonçalo de Ocampo."[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources 
2 CONT Journal of the Peruvian Institute of Genealogical Research, Issues 4-6, Publisher El Instituto, 1949, University of Texas. ↑ Agreda y Sánchez, José María de., Dorantes de Carranza, Baltasar., González Obregón, Luis., Ramírez, José
2 CONC  Fernando. Summary account of the affairs of New Spain: with individual information on the legitimate descendants of the Spanish conquerors and first settlers. Spain: Impr. from the National Museum, 1902.
